From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 11:45:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/8] Add QuickJSInstance: several VMs in one WASM instance

Each QuickJS.create() instantiates the WASM module anew, so a host that
runs many VMs (one per plugin, say) pays for one linear memory per VM,
and that memory is never returned to the next VM.

QuickJSInstance.create({ wasm, wasi?, extensions? }) instantiates the
module once; inst.newVm(options) creates a VM in it, with the options of
QuickJS.create() except those of the instance. Each VM has its own
JSRuntime: its own heap, memory limit, interrupt handler, module loader,
rejection handler, timezone, host functions and GC. The VMs share the
linear memory, the WASI imports, the extensions (initialized in every
VM) and the native stack. vm.dispose() frees the runtime, and the next
VM reuses its memory; inst.dispose() drops the instance.

In interface.c, a VM (QJSVm) holds its runtime and context, and the
memory accounting that was global: the VM is the opaque of the
runtime's malloc functions, so each runtime is charged for its own
allocations whatever VM is current, and one VM running out of memory
does not affect another. The runtime's opaque points back to its VM.
qjs_vm_new() creates a VM, qjs_vm_select() makes it the one the other
exports act on (they keep using the rt/ctx globals), qjs_vm_free()
frees it. qjs_init()/qjs_init2()/qjs_destroy() and
qjs_set_runtime_and_context() work as before, on top of these.

The host imports pass the VM they come from, and the host dispatches to
it. A host function of one VM may call into another VM, whose host
functions may call back: every trampoline saves the current VM before
calling the import and restores it after, and the host keeps its own
notion of the current VM in sync (restoring it itself when an import
throws). The exports of a VM of a shared instance select the VM lazily,
only when another one is current; the VM of QuickJS.create() owns its
instance and uses the exports directly, as before.

The boxes that carry JSValues to the host are linked into the list of
their VM, so that qjs_vm_free() releases the values of handles the host
never disposed: JS_FreeRuntime() asserts that no object is left, and a
leaked handle would otherwise abort the whole instance.

A VM cannot be disposed while it is running (from its own host
functions or handlers). snapshot() captures the whole memory, so it
throws for a VM of a shared instance unless it is the only VM there.
